//BUG="a void value bound to a variable emits `void var0;`, which gcc rejects"
//OUT="ok"

// `void` is a type the compiler accepts in a binding position, so a call to a
// function that returns nothing can be bound to a variable. Codegen then gives
// that variable storage:
//
//     void var0;
//     var0 = func1(1);
//
// and gcc rejects it with "variable has incomplete type 'void'" — a compile
// that produces C which does not build is the worst of both worlds, because
// the error surfaces from a tool the user did not run.
//
// A void variable has nothing to hold, so it should have no storage in C at
// all: the declaration is dropped and the assignment collapses to the bare
// call `func1(1);`. The other cases numbered 25-47 here cover the same erasure
// in every other position a value can appear in.
//
// This case used to assert the opposite — that the compiler should reject the
// binding outright. Binding is now the intended behaviour, so it asserts that
// the program builds and runs instead.
//
// 05_functions/66e_using_the_value_of_a_void_function.cpl and
// 05_functions/73e_printing_a_bound_void_value.cpl pin down the other side of
// this: erasing the storage must not make a void value usable as a value.

fn nothing x
    ret

fn main
    r = nothing(1)
    out "ok"
